<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lüshunkou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Port Arthur, Ryojun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lüshunkou, known to Europeans as Port Arthur, is a port at the tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> peninsula in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> leased it from the Qing in 1898 as an ice-free naval base, prompting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to take Weihaiwei. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> captured it after a long siege in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904–1905 and held it as part of the Kwantung Leased Territory until 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lushunkou.jpg" alt="Lüshunkou" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%BCshunkouPrison-EntranceGate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lüshunkou lies at the extreme southern tip of the Liaodong Peninsula, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> to the south-east, Korea Bay to the east and the Bohai Sea to the west. Its inner natural harbour, a lake-like basin, was well sheltered and easy to fortify in the eyes of nineteenth-century planners. The name means port of smooth journeys and was given in 1371 to commemorate a Ming expedition that sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengzhou">Penglai</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and landed there to attack the remaining Yuan forces. English-language writers called it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-arthur-tasmania">Port Arthur</a> after Lieutenant William Arthur of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, who surveyed the harbour in 1860.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 1880s the German company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> was contracted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> to build fortifications around the port. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>, on 20 and 21 November 1894, 15,000 Japanese troops defeated 13,000 Qing soldiers and took the town. The Port Arthur massacre that followed killed between 2,600 civilians and 20,000 people, including Chinese soldiers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was granted the peninsula by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> in 1895 but had to give it up under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-intervention">Triple Intervention</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which was seen in Japan as a great humiliation.</p><p>In 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> obtained a lease of the peninsula and a railway right of way linking it to the Chinese Eastern Railway, with a line from Port Arthur to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>. Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> believed a Pacific port would strengthen Russian security and economic influence.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> attacked the port and the Russian fleet without a declaration of war on 9 February 1904, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>. The port fell on 2 January 1905 after the Siege of Port Arthur. Japan then took over the Kwantung Leased Territory and renamed the town Ryojun, and the railway became part of the South Manchurian Railway. After Japan's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> administered the port, which it rented from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, until 1950. Soviet troops stayed until 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chinese Lüshun City was set up on 25 November 1945. On 7 January 1960 it became Lüshunkou District, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalian">Dalian</a> in Liaoning Province, which was known as Lüda until 1981. The district covers 512.15 square kilometres. Japanese-era war monuments stand on 203 Hill and Baiyu Mountain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lüshunkou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Russo-Japanese War of 1904–1905 Russia lost both its Pacific squadron at <b>Port Arthur</b> and the Baltic Fleet, annihilated at the Battle of Tsushima in May 1905 after a voyage of some 18,000 nautical miles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After capturing <b>Lüshunkou</b>, known to Europeans as Port Arthur, advance troops of the Japanese Second Army began three days of killing in the city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Portsmouth in 1905 Japan took over Russia's lease of Kwantung Oblast, including <b>Port Arthur</b> (Ryojun) and Dalny (Dairen), together with the railway south of Changchun.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-leased-territory">Kwantung Leased Territory</a></li><li><blockquote>Russia fortified <b>Port Arthur</b> as an ice-free Pacific naval base and built a commercial port at Dalny.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-oblast">Kwantung Oblast</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1898, after Russia leased <b>Port Arthur</b>, Britain obtained Weihaiwei from the Qing Empire on a lease to last as long as Russia held Port Arthur.</blockquote><a href="/state/weihaiwei">Weihaiwei</a></li></ul></section>
